.TH TRIER-BRIDGE 1 "2026-09-21" "trier-bridge 0.1.0~dev1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
trier-bridge \- Windows-to-Linux experience compatibility layer
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B trier-bridge
.RI [ \-\-version ]
.RI [ \-\-verbose ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Trier Bridge
gives Windows users a familiar place for the things they already know how to do
on Linux: files, apps, settings, printers, network, and familiar troubleshooting
tools such as Task Manager, Event Viewer, Device Manager, Disk Management, and
Services. Linux stays authoritative underneath. The application runs entirely as
the signed-in user; it installs no daemon and no privileged helper. Privileged
operations, when they exist, go through the system's own polkit-mediated
services and prompt the way GNOME Settings does.
.PP
This is a development build. Views that are not implemented say so and change
nothing on the computer.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-verbose
Also write the log to standard error.
.SH FILES
Trier Bridge writes only under its own per-user directories:
.TP
.I ~/.config/trier-bridge/
preferences and the integration ledger
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/trier-bridge/logs/
bounded, redacted log files
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/trier-bridge/journal/
operation journal and recovery records
.PP
Removing the package does not delete these per-user files; the application
offers "Remove all integrations" and the directories above can be deleted by
the user.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
Standard XDG base directory variables are honoured.
